para.example:
#include <stdio.h>

#include "tjs.h"

#include "tjsError.h"



int main(int argc, char* argv[])

{

	setlocale(LC_ALL, ""); // Set locale



	tTJS *tjsengine = new tTJS(); // Create TJS2 script engine



	try

	{

		tTJSVariant result; // Variable to receive the result



		tjsengine->ExecScript(

			TJS_W(

				"function test(x, y) { return x*y; } \n"

				"return test(4, 5);\n"),

				&result, NULL,

				TJS_W("test code"));	// Execute test script



		printf("Result : %d\n", (int)result); // Display result

	}

	catch(eTJSError &e)

	{

		printf("An error occurred : %ls\n", e.GetMessage().c_str());

	}

	catch(...)

	{

		printf("An error occurred\n");

	}



	tjsengine->Shutdown(); // Shutdown TJS2 script engine

	tjsengine->Release(); // Release TJS2 script engine



	return 0;

}

doc.para:
Native Classes
　TJS2 has a mechanism for handling native classes written in languages like C++.
　Objects of type iTJSNativeInstance, called native instances, can be registered with each object (iTJSDispatch2 interface) and retrieved from the object.
　Native instances are identified by a unique class ID, and when creating a native class, a class ID must be obtained.

　However, macros for performing these operations are defined in tjsNative.h, so using them is easy.
　The following example implements a simple class using these macros.

　First is the implementation of the native instance. To implement a native instance, derive a class from tTJSNativeInstance. tTJSNativeInstance is a class implemented in tjsNative.cpp / tjsNative.h that implements the basic behavior of iTJSNativeInstance.



class NI_Test : public tTJSNativeInstance // Native instance

{

public:

	NI_Test()

	{

		// Constructor

		Value = 0;

	}



	tjs_error TJS_INTF_METHOD

		Construct(tjs_int numparams, tTJSVariant **param, iTJSDispatch2 *tjs_obj)

	{

		// Called when TJS2 object is created



		// If arguments exist, set them as the initial value in Value

		if(numparams >= 1 && param[0]->Type() != tvtVoid)

			Value = (tjs_int)*param[0];



		return S_OK;

	}



	void TJS_INTF_METHOD Invalidate()

	{

		// Called when the object is invalidated

	}



	void SetValue(tjs_int n) { Value = n; }

	tjs_int GetValue() const { return Value; }



	tjs_int GetSquare() const { return Value*Value; }

	void Add(tjs_int n) { Value += n; }

	void Print() const { printf("%d\n", Value); }



private:

	tjs_int Value; // Value

};




Line 35

　Out of order, but here are the data members. In a native instance, you can freely write necessary data members.



Lines 4 to 8

　Constructor for NI_Test. It is recommended to perform C++ class initialization here rather than in Construct described later, keeping initialization in Construct to a minimum.
　In this example, the data member Value is set to 0 as an initial value.




Lines 10 to 20

　Called when a TJS2 object is created with the new operator. The numparams and param arguments represent the arguments passed to the new operator.
　The tjs_obj argument is the TJS object being created.
　In this example, if there is an argument (and it is not void), it is set as the initial value of Value.




Lines 22 to 25

　Method called when the object is invalidated. Cleanup logic should be written here.
　In this example, it does nothing.




Lines 27 to 32

　Public methods for manipulating data members. Code utilizing these will be written inside the native class described later.




　Since a class is required to create objects, we define the class. The class is implemented by deriving from tTJSNativeClass. tTJSNativeClass has the iTJSDispatch2 interface, implementing basic behavior to act as a native class.
　Methods and properties accessible from TJS are written inside the native class constructor.



class NC_Test : public tTJSNativeClass // Native class

{

public:

	NC_Test(); // Constructor; described below



	static tjs_uint32 ClassID; // Class ID



private:

	tTJSNativeInstance *CreateNativeInstance()

	{

		return new NI_Test(); // Create and return native instance

	}

};

tjs_uint32 NC_Test::ClassID = (tjs_uint32)-1; // Class ID




Line 4

　Constructor of this class. Implementation is described below.




Line 6

　Variable to hold the class ID of this class. The instance entity is at line 14.




Lines 9 to 12

　The CreateNativeInstance method is called when a native instance needs to be created. Here, an object of the NI_Test class is created and returned.









NC_Test::NC_Test() : tTJSNativeClass(TJS_W("Test"))

{

	TJS_BEGIN_NATIVE_MEMBERS(/*TJS class name*/Test)



		TJS_DECL_EMPTY_FINALIZE_METHOD



		TJS_BEGIN_NATIVE_CONSTRUCTOR_DECL(

			/*var.name*/_this,

			/*var.type*/NI_Test,

			/*TJS class name*/Test)

		{

			// Content can also be written in NI_Test::Construct,

			// so nothing is done here

			return TJS_S_OK;

		}

		TJS_END_NATIVE_CONSTRUCTOR_DECL(/*TJS class name*/Test)



		TJS_BEGIN_NATIVE_METHOD_DECL(/*func. name*/print) // print method

		{

			TJS_GET_NATIVE_INSTANCE(/*var. name*/_this,

				/*var. type*/NI_Test);



			_this->Print();



			return TJS_S_OK;

		}

		TJS_END_NATIVE_METHOD_DECL(/*func. name*/print)



		TJS_BEGIN_NATIVE_METHOD_DECL(/*func. name*/add) // add method

		{

			TJS_GET_NATIVE_INSTANCE(/*var. name*/_this,

				/*var. type*/NI_Test);



			if(numparams < 1) return TJS_E_BADPARAMCOUNT;



			_this->Add((tjs_int)*param[0]);



			return TJS_S_OK;

		}

		TJS_END_NATIVE_METHOD_DECL(/*func. name*/add)



		TJS_BEGIN_NATIVE_PROP_DECL(value) // value property

		{

			TJS_BEGIN_NATIVE_PROP_GETTER

			{

				TJS_GET_NATIVE_INSTANCE(/*var. name*/_this,

					/*var. type*/NI_Test);

				*result = _this->GetValue();

				return TJS_S_OK;

			}

			TJS_END_NATIVE_PROP_GETTER



			TJS_BEGIN_NATIVE_PROP_SETTER

			{

				TJS_GET_NATIVE_INSTANCE(/*var. name*/_this,

					/*var. type*/NI_Test);

				_this->SetValue((tjs_int)*param);

				return TJS_S_OK;

			}

			TJS_END_NATIVE_PROP_SETTER

		}

		TJS_END_NATIVE_PROP_DECL(value)



		TJS_BEGIN_NATIVE_PROP_DECL(square) // square read-only property

		{

			TJS_BEGIN_NATIVE_PROP_GETTER

			{

				TJS_GET_NATIVE_INSTANCE(/*var. name*/_this,

					/*var. type*/NI_Test);



				*result = _this->GetSquare();



				return TJS_S_OK;

			}

			TJS_END_NATIVE_PROP_GETTER



			TJS_DENY_NATIVE_PROP_SETTER

		}

		TJS_END_NATIVE_PROP_DECL(square)



	TJS_END_NATIVE_MEMBERS

}




Line 1

　Constructor of NC_Test. In the constructor of the parent class tTJSNativeClass, specify the class name used in TJS2.




Line 3

　The TJS_BEGIN_NATIVE_MEMBERS macro. Specify the class name used in TJS2 as the argument.
　Methods and properties that should be members of the class are written between this macro and the TJS_END_NATIVE_MEMBERS macro.




Line 4

　Declaring an empty finalize method. Processing corresponding to finalize can also be implemented by overriding tTJSNativeInstance::Invalidate, so normally an empty method is sufficient.





Lines 7 to 16

　Declaring the (TJS) constructor. When writing a class in TJS, this corresponds to declaring a method with the same name as the class inside the class.

　The 1st argument of the TJS_BEGIN_NATIVE_CONSTRUCTOR_DECL macro is the variable name assigned to the native instance, and the 2nd argument is the type name of that variable. Inside this block in this example, a variable named NI_Test * _this is available to access the native instance.
　The 3rd argument of the macro specifies the class name used in TJS. The argument of the TJS_END_NATIVE_CONSTRUCTOR_DECL macro is similar.
　Here too, processing corresponding to the constructor can be implemented by overriding tTJSNativeInstance::Construct, so nothing is done here and S_OK is returned.





Lines 18 to 27

　Declaring the print method. The method name must be specified identically in both TJS_BEGIN_NATIVE_METHOD_DECL and TJS_END_NATIVE_METHOD_DECL macros.
　Variables available inside this macro include tjs_int numparams and tTJSVariant **param, indicating the number of passed arguments and the arguments themselves, respectively. They are not used in this method.
　Lines 20 to 21 are macros for retrieving the native instance from the object. In this example, it means retrieving the native instance into a variable named _this of type NI_Test *. From then on, the native instance can be accessed via the variable _this. Line 23 calls the Print method of that native instance.




Lines 29 to 40

　Declaring the add method. numparams and param are used here.





Lines 42 to 62

　Declaring the value property. Specify the property name in both TJS_BEGIN_NATIVE_PROP_DECL and TJS_END_NATIVE_PROP_DECL macros, just like method declarations.

　A getter can be written in the area enclosed by TJS_BEGIN_NATIVE_PROP_GETTER and TJS_END_NATIVE_PROP_GETTER macros. Inside the getter, write code to set the value in *result which is of tTJSVariant type.
　Similarly, a setter can be written in the area enclosed by TJS_BEGIN_NATIVE_PROP_SETTER and TJS_END_NATIVE_PROP_SETTER macros. Inside the setter, the value to be set is stored in *param of tTJSVariant type, so process using that.




Lines 64 to 79

　Declaring a read-only property here. A read-only property can be created by writing TJS_DENY_NATIVE_PROP_SETTER instead of a setter.






　Registering a native class is the same as registering a native function. An example test code is shown below.



		iTJSDispatch2 * global = tjsengine->GetGlobalNoAddRef();

			// Get global object



		iTJSDispatch2 *cls = new NC_Test(); // Create NC_Test object

		tTJSVariant cls_var(cls); // Set object in tTJSVariant cls_var

		cls->Release(); // Release cls



		TJS_THROW_IF_ERROR(

			global->PropSet(TJS_MEMBERENSURE, TJS_W("Test"), NULL, &cls_var, NULL));

				// Register



		tjsengine->ExecScript(TJS_W(

			"var test = new Test();\n"

			"test.value = 5;\n"

			"var test2 = new Test(test.square);\n"

			"test2.add(3);\n"

			"test2.print();\n\0"),

				NULL, NULL, NULL); // Execute script
